format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: inside-the-atom

lesson stable-and-unstable-nuclei
title: Stable and unstable nuclei
minutes: 4
xp: 50
teaches: u1-stable-unstable
teaches: u1-unstable-changes
relies_on: u1-nucleus
relies_on: u1-isotope-definition

description:
    Some nuclei stay the same for ever. Others are unstable and can change. You will see the difference, learn what radioactivity is, and find out that it is a natural thing.

cite ref-openstax-up3-ch10
supports: Isotopes of carbon, hydrogen and other elements, stable or with a half-life.

cite ref-epa-radioactive-decay
supports: Stable and unstable forms of an element; unstable forms are radioactive.

cite ref-hps-natural-radioactivity
supports: Natural potassium-40 in the human body.

card c01
kind: item
type: item
item: u1-l5-predict-stable
scored: 0

card c02
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Stable and unstable
figure: stable-vs-unstable
figure_at: 1

body:
    Some combinations of protons and neutrons make a stable nucleus. It stays the same. Others make an unstable nucleus, which can change. Most atoms around you, and in you, are stable.

card c03
kind: sim
type: atom-builder
scored: 0
task: Carbon-14 is unstable. Change the neutrons until the carbon atom is stable.
hint: Stable carbon has 6 or 7 neutrons. Tap - until the badge says Stable.

config:
    {
      "mode": "stability",
      "goal": {
        "stable": true
      },
      "start": {
        "protons": 6,
        "neutrons": 8,
        "electrons": 6
      },
      "lock": {
        "protons": true,
        "electrons": true
      },
      "maxNeutrons": 12,
      "objectives": [
        "u1-stable-unstable"
      ]
    }

card c04
kind: item
type: item
item: u1-l5-sort-stable

card c05
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Unstable nuclei can change
figure: decay-figure
figure_at: 1

body:
    An unstable nucleus can change. When it does, it gives off [[radiation]]. This is called [[radioactivity]]. An atom that does this is radioactive.

card c06
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Radioactivity is natural
figure: natural-sources
figure_at: 1

body:
    Nobody had to invent radioactivity. Unstable atoms are in rocks, in the air and in food. Bananas and your own body hold a little natural potassium-40.

